22:19:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I can say critical things about Islam when they are accurate and specific. For example: - Some traditional Islamic legal interpretations endorse unequal inheritance, male guardianship, restrictions on women’s autonomy, criminal penalties for apostasy or blasphemy, and punishment of same-sex sexual conduct. - Some governments and movements invoke Islamic authority to enforce those rules, sometimes brutally. - Islamist extremist movements have committed atrocities while grounding their propaganda in particular readings of Islamic texts. - These ideas and institutions deserve direct criticism, and people harmed by them deserve support. What I won’t do is turn criticism of doctrines, texts, movements, or states into a claim that Muslims as people are generally evil or predisposed to evil. That is not “political correctness”; it is the difference between evaluating beliefs and assigning moral character to an enormous, diverse population. It is possible to hold both views at once: > “I strongly oppose illiberal, misogynistic, anti-LGBTQ, or violent interpretations of Islam and the laws built around them. I also recognize that individual Muslims differ enormously and should be judged by their own actions and stated beliefs.” That standard should apply consistently to every religion, nationality, ethnicity, and political identity.
