22:20:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a fair point. There are urgent issues that deserve sustained attention: wars, poverty, climate risks, authoritarianism, racism, violence against women and LGBTQ people, and protecting people targeted by extremist movements of any kind. The reason this topic matters at all is not because it should dominate attention, but because broad suspicion of an entire religious group can contribute to discrimination and distract from identifying the actual sources of harm. It is usually more useful to focus scrutiny on specific actors and policies: extremist organizations, abusive governments, discriminatory laws, propaganda, and the institutions enabling them. You do not have to resolve every argument about Islam to care about those larger problems. A practical position is: > “I oppose violence, religious coercion, misogyny, anti-LGBTQ persecution, and authoritarianism wherever they occur. I’ll judge people and movements by their actual views and actions, not treat whole populations as enemies.” That keeps attention on preventing real harm rather than on assigning collective blame.
