22:24:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may be a natural instinct, but natural instincts can still be unreliable—especially when they turn limited, vivid examples into a judgment about millions or billions of strangers. Separating doctrine, institutions, and individuals is not artificial; it is how you make your judgment more accurate: - A doctrine can endorse an idea that many followers reject or reinterpret. - A government can impose rules that many citizens oppose. - An extremist movement can claim to represent a faith while killing members of that faith. - A person can identify with a religion for family, culture, spirituality, or community while disagreeing with parts of its tradition. You can still recognize common patterns where evidence supports them. Just name the pattern precisely: “support for X policy is higher in Y surveyed population,” or “this movement promotes Z.” That is very different from treating a broad religious identity as evidence that an unfamiliar person has bad character or is likely to harm you. The careful distinction is not a demand to ignore reality. It is a way to avoid mistaking a stereotype for a useful assessment of actual people.
